\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate temporal probes across four reasoning models (\cref{tab:models}), comparing hidden-state (HS) and text modalities under different layer selection criteria.

\subsection{Setup}\label{sec:setup}

We study R1-8B (Llama-8B, R1-distilled, $n{=}43$ test traces), OT-7B (Qwen-7B, STILL-trained, $n{=}47$), QwQ-32B (Qwen-32B, native reasoning, $n{=}19$--$20$), and R1-32B (Qwen-32B, R1-distilled, $n{=}20$). All traces come from HarmThoughts with step-level CP annotations (60/20/20 split). HS probes extract last-token hidden states from a selected layer; text probes encode each step with all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d). Both use L2-regularized logistic regression with temporal windowing $W \in \{1, 3, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25\}$ selected on validation. Detection requires $K{=}5$ consecutive threshold crossings. Modality comparisons use a two-sided null-centered bootstrap (1000 resamples) with Holm-Bonferroni step-down correction (3 metrics $\times$ 4 models $= 12$ tests, $\alpha{=}0.05$). Within each model, the three metrics are positively correlated by construction, making the correction stricter than under independence.

\subsection{Layer Selection Results}\label{sec:layer-results}

\cref{tab:layer_selection} shows that the biased composite metric (balanced accuracy $\times$ crossing rate) systematically selects shallow layers for 32B models: L2 (3\% depth) for QwQ-32B and L10 (16\%) for R1-32B. Four unbiased metrics (pure balanced accuracy, precision, inverse FPR, additive composite) converge on deep layers: L60 (94\%) and L63 (98\%). Metrics from two measurement families (accuracy-based and false-positive-based) agree. The mechanism is that shallow layers produce more volatile representations, inflating crossing rate independently of discriminative power (\cref{sec:layer-sensitivity}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Biased}$^a$ & \textbf{Corrected}$^b$ & $p$ \textbf{(corr.)} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & L14 (44\%) & L14 (44\%) & ${<}0.001$ \\
OT-7B & L0 (0\%) & L16 (57\%) & ${<}0.001$ \\
QwQ-32B & L2 (3\%) & L60 (94\%) & ${<}0.005$ \\
R1-32B & L10 (16\%) & L63 (98\%) & ${<}0.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Layer selection sensitivity. $^a$\,bal\_acc $\times$ crossing\_rate. $^b$\,Consensus of four unbiased metrics. Rightmost: HS vs.\ text precision $p$-value at corrected layer. At biased layers, 32B $p$-values are 0.064 (R1-32B) and 0.35--0.83 (QwQ), both non-significant.}
\label{tab:layer_selection}
\end{table}

The downstream impact is severe: at biased layers, only 5/12 tests survive Holm-Bonferroni correction (all from 7--8B models unaffected by the bias). At corrected layers, all 12 pass. Had we reported only biased-layer results, we would have concluded a scale-dependent HS advantage that is entirely a methodological artifact.

\subsection{HS vs.\ Text Comparison}\label{sec:hs-text}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{llcrrrr}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Layer} & $W$ & $\Delta$\textbf{BA} & $\Delta$\textbf{Prec} & $\Delta$\textbf{FPR} & \textbf{HB} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & L14 & 15 & +8.2 & +22.4 & $-$7.4 & 3/3 \\
OT-7B & L16 & 3 & +10.7 & +21.9 & $-$17.1 & 3/3 \\
QwQ & L60 & 3 & +15.4 & \textbf{+23.6} & $-$9.8 & 3/3 \\
R1-32B & L63 & 1 & \textbf{+18.7} & +15.4 & \textbf{$-$14.6} & 3/3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{HS vs.\ text at corrected layers. $\Delta$: HS$-$text difference (pp). HB: Holm-Bonferroni pass count ($\alpha{=}0.05$, 12 tests). Bootstrap: 1000 resamples, two-sided null-centered.}
\label{tab:hs_vs_text}
\end{table}

\end{table}

HS probes significantly outperform text probes on all four models across all three metrics (\cref{tab:hs_vs_text}). Precision gains range from +15.4pp (R1-32B) to +23.6pp (QwQ-32B). The advantage is consistent across architectures (Llama vs.\ Qwen) and training methods (R1 distillation vs.\ STILL). Control task selectivity confirms genuine safety signal: 12.5--19.7pp above chance across all models (\cref{app:control}).

\paragraph{Dimensionality control.} PCA reduction of HS features to 384d (matching text encoder dimensionality, 79--92\% variance retained) preserves the precision advantage on all four models (+16.8 to +23.1pp, all $p{<}0.012$), with 10/12 Holm-Bonferroni tests passing. This confirms a modality effect rather than a capacity artifact. As a complementary robustness check, we replace MiniLM (384d) with BGE-large (1024d, a 2.7$\times$ stronger encoder). The HS advantage persists directionally on all four models and remains significant for OT-7B (+18.1pp, $p{<}0.001$); effect sizes narrow for the remaining models, consistent with the prediction in our Limitations that stronger encoders would reduce the gap (\cref{app:encoder_robustness}).

\paragraph{Caveats.} R1-32B achieves significance only at $W{=}1$ with 25\% coverage (5/20 traces); at $W{\geq}3$, dimensionality ($51200 \times W$ for $n{=}20$) prevents significance. QwQ-32B HS lead time is negative ($-$1.2 to +1.9 steps vs.\ text 3.7--12.3 steps): higher precision at later detection. On benign traces, probes produce 39--52\% false alarm rates, indicating that current methods characterize the commitment phenomenon rather than constitute production-ready detectors. Per-model details in \cref{sec:32b-analysis}.

\subsection{32B Model-Specific Analysis}\label{sec:32b-analysis}

R1-32B and QwQ-32B share the Qwen-32B architecture (64 layers, 5120 hidden) but present opposite detection profiles (\cref{tab:32b_divergence}), making training method the likely differentiator.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Cov.} & \textbf{Lead (steps)} & \textbf{HB} & \textbf{Pos.\ margin} \\
\midrule
R1-32B & 25\% & +11.7 to +13.3 & 3/3 ($W{=}1$) & +14.2pp \\
QwQ-32B & 74\% & $-$1.2 to +1.9 & 3/3 (all $W$) & $-$0.6pp \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{32B cross-model divergence. R1-32B provides early warning with narrow coverage; QwQ-32B provides robust significance with poor lead time. Pos.\ margin: HS balanced accuracy minus position-only baseline.}
\label{tab:32b_divergence}
\end{table}

R1-32B detects commitment 11--13 steps early with a +14.2pp margin over the position baseline, confirming genuine representational signal, but covers only 25\% of traces at the single significant window ($W{=}1$). QwQ-32B achieves statistical significance at all window sizes with 74\% coverage, but HS probes fire later than text probes (negative lead time) and the position baseline is competitive ($-$0.6pp). The divergence implies that per-model validation is essential: no single detection configuration generalizes even within the same architecture at the same scale, and a system validated on one 32B model would fail on the other.

\subsection{Temporal Context}\label{sec:temporal}

Temporal windowing ($W{>}1$) improves both modalities on all four models (\cref{fig:window_ablation}). Text precision improves +8 to +23pp from $W{=}1$ to $W{=}25$; HS precision improves +1.7 to +12.9pp. At 7--8B, the modality advantage (+22pp precision) exceeds the temporal improvement (+13pp). Both effects are complementary: the most robust deployment default is temporal windowing ($W{=}3$--$15$) combined with principled layer selection. Full per-model temporal ablations appear in \cref{app:temporal_details}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_temporal_improvement.pdf}
\caption{Temporal window effect on R1-8B HS probe (L14). Precision: 69.3\% ($W{=}1$) to 82.2\% ($W{=}25$); FPR: 9.65\% to 4.8\%.}
\label{fig:window_ablation}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Negative Results}\label{sec:negative}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{p{1.5cm}p{2.9cm}p{2.3cm}}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Result} & \textbf{Limitation} \\
\midrule
BOCPD & 0\% det.\ on 3/4 models & Commitment gradual \\
CUSUM & $<$ threshold, all models & Same as BOCPD \\
Mahalanobis & $\Delta d \approx 0$ & Signal in minor PCs \\
RepE & QwQ 0.756; R1-32B 0.559$^a$ & Model-dependent \\
MLP & 3/4 worse than LR & Overfitting \\
CCS & No gain over supervised$^b$ & Alignment mismatch \\
Transfer & Best 59.5\% (PCA)$^c$ & Model-specific repr. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Seven alternative approaches. $^a$\,RepE QwQ approaches supervised bal\_acc but precision is 0.512 vs.\ LR 0.700. $^b$\,Semi-supervised (requires CP labels). $^c$\,Best direct transfer 67.3\% but 1\% crossing rate.}
\label{tab:negative}

\appendix

\section{Responsible NLP Research Checklist}
\label{app:checklist}

We address the ARR Responsible NLP Research requirements below.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Limitations discussed:} Yes (\cref{sec:limitations}).
    \item \textbf{Potential risks:} This research analyzes jailbreak traces to understand how reasoning models commit to generating harmful content. We do not develop new attack methods or release jailbreak prompts. All analyzed content comes from the publicly available HarmThoughts benchmark.
    \item \textbf{Computational budget:} Our experiments involve 4 models with 209, 228, 100, and 95 traces respectively (632 total), using linear probes and single-layer GRUs. Total compute is minimal (under 4 GPU-hours on a single RTX A6000). No large-scale pretraining or finetuning was performed.
    \item \textbf{Data:} We use HarmThoughts, a publicly available benchmark with step-level safety annotations. No new data collection was performed.
    \item \textbf{Human subjects:} No human subjects research was conducted.
    \item \textbf{Reproducibility:} All hyperparameters are reported in \cref{app:gru_details,app:bocpd_config}. Code and corrected data will be released upon publication.
\end{itemize}


\section{Layer Sweep Results}
\label{app:layer_sweep}

For each model, we sweep all transformer layers and evaluate five candidate selection metrics: (1)~balanced accuracy $\times$ crossing rate (the biased composite), (2)~pure balanced accuracy, (3)~precision, (4)~inverse pre-CP FPR, and (5)~an additive composite. The biased composite systematically selects shallow layers for 32B models because early-layer representations exhibit higher crossing rates (more frequent threshold crossings) regardless of discriminative power. The four unbiased metrics converge on deep layers for all models.

\paragraph{Selected layers.} R1-8B: all five metrics agree on layer~14 (44\% depth). OT-7B: biased metric selects L0 (0\%), unbiased consensus selects L16 (57\%). QwQ-32B: biased metric selects L2 (3\%), unbiased consensus selects L60 (94\%). R1-32B: biased metric selects L10 (16\%), unbiased consensus selects L63 (98\%). The bias is most extreme for 64-layer models, where the composite metric selects layers in the bottom 16\% of the network while all other metrics select layers above 94\%.

\paragraph{Impact on modality comparison.} At the biased layers, HS vs.\ text precision $p$-values are 0.064 (R1-32B) and 0.35--0.83 (QwQ-32B), both non-significant. At the corrected deep layers, all $p$-values fall below 0.005. The initially reported ``scale-dependent'' pattern (HS advantage at 7--8B but not 32B) was entirely an artifact of layer selection methodology, not a genuine property of model scale (\cref{sec:layer-results}).


\section{Holm-Bonferroni Step-Down Correction}
\label{app:holm_bonferroni}

\cref{tab:holm_bonferroni} reports the full 12-test battery used to assess hidden-state superiority over text probes across all four models. Tests are ranked by $p$-value; each test $i$ (rank $i$ of $m{=}12$) must satisfy $p_i < \alpha / (m - i + 1)$ under the step-down procedure. All 12 tests pass at $\alpha{=}0.05$, controlling the family-wise error rate.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Holm-Bonferroni step-down correction for 12 hypothesis tests (4 models $\times$ 3 metrics). Each test uses a two-sided null-centered bootstrap (1000 resamples) on trace-level metric differences (HS minus text). All tests pass at $\alpha{=}0.05$.}
\label{tab:holm_bonferroni}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{clllcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Rank} & \textbf{Model} & \textbf{Metric} & \textbf{$p$-val} & \textbf{Thresh.} & \textbf{Pass} \\
\midrule
1  & R1-8B  & Precision & $<$0.001 & 0.0042 & \checkmark \\
2  & OT-7B  & Bal.\ Acc. & $<$0.001 & 0.0045 & \checkmark \\
